thread:
maria: Quick check-in on the runtime-side fairness hooks we prototyped last week. thinking about where to draw the boundary between router-level admission and runtime-level pacing.

jason: +1. main tradeoff i keep circling is accurate token accounting vs scheduling granularity. router has global view but looses per-GPU slot context. runtime has slot details but only sees requests as they arrive.

arjun: Also memory/KV cache contention if we delay in runtime. If we admit too many short-lived bursts into the runtime for fairness rebalancing, we blow up memory with many KV contexts.
emily: Could we do a lightweight early-reject at router with a soft token reservation and then let runtime finish exact accounting? i.e., router returns provisional admit id and runtime can later convert -> commit/release.
maria: that's the provisional reservation idea. implementation notes: +1 to reserving small credit slices at router (cheap), and runtime consumes them and reports back delta. Concern: latencies for short requests where the roundtrip commit happens after response.
jason: For short requests we can pre-charge router and mark them as finalized on success. Only long-running requests need a follow-up reconciliation. That minimizes extra RTT.
arjun: We should define error cases: if runtime OOMs and evicts a KV cache, we may need to refund provisional credits. Need a deterministic reconciliation protocol.
emaria: agree. proto sketch (very small):
```
message ProvisionalReserve {
  string reserve_id = 1;
  string tenant_id = 2;
  int64 tokens_reserved = 3;
  int64 ttl_ms = 4;
}
message ReservationResult {
  string reserve_id = 1;
  bool committed = 2;
  int64 tokens_used = 3;
  string reason = 4;
}
```
arjun: looks fine. ttl_ms is important to avoid stale holds. router should auto-expire provisional reserves.
emily: Metric-wise we need three things: provisional_reserve_count, commit_success_rate, commit_refund_tokens. Otherwise ops can't debug leaks.
ops-bot: deploy-bake: runtime/fairhook v0.2 rolled to staging at 09:12 UTC.
maria: performance question — when runtime rejects and refunds, router must be able to retry to alternative capacity tier (e.g., dedicated -> hosted fallback). do we want router to auto-retry or surface to caller?
jason: auto-retry on cheap fallbacks (one attempt) is ok but must be bounded. add exponential backoff jitter.
emaria: edge case: multi-chunk streaming responses that hold KV state across chunks. If router reserved per-request tokens and runtime later extends usage across chunks, the commit step must be able to reference the original reserve. we need a stable request id.
arjun: for streaming, reserve upfront using an estimated upper bound, then runtime delta-report after each chunk. may be heavy but safer. we can add heuristic: use observed chunk distribution to shrink future reservations.
emily: sounds like we need an adaptive estimator in router that learns per-tenant chunk distributions. tie it into Optimize recommendations later.
maria: ops question — how do we surface misbehaving tenants that repeatedly get refunded? we want an abuse score signal out of this flow. maybe router increments an abuse counter when refunds exceed X% within window.
jason: yeah, abuse_score += refunded_tokens / reserved_tokens * weight, decays over time. keep it simple initially.
emaria: actionable items: 1) arjun to write reconciliation failure cases + state machine. 2) jason to prototype reserve & commit gRPC endpoints. 3) emily to add metrics list and dashboard mockups. 4) maria to draft RFC addendum with sequence diagrams.
emaria: one more note — prefer idempotent commit RPCs: commit(reserve_id, tokens_used) must be safe to retry. make reserve_id opaque GUID from router.
arjun: will start a short doc with state transitions (reserved -> committed -> refunded -> expired) and race conditions.
emaria: thanks all. If no objections i'll push a small staging rollout tomorrow and capture telemetry. ping @jason @arjun if you want to pair on first deploy. :thumbsup:
